IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea-cpc-interface`
IMPORT `ea-types`

-- Evidence Act 1893, Part 1: admissions and confessions (sections 17 to 23, 31) and statements of
-- relevant facts by persons who are not called (sections 32 to 33).
-- Source: source/EA1893.txt lines 1059 to 1600.
--
-- The question this module answers: IS THIS STATEMENT RELEVANT, AND SO ADMISSIBLE, UNDER THE
-- EVIDENCE ACT?  A statement is dealt with by the person who made it and the route it comes in
-- by: an admission or confession (17 to 23), a statement by a person who is dead, cannot be found
-- or the like (32), or testimony given earlier (33).  The hearsay exceptions of section 32 are the
-- Act's own statutory hearsay framework; the Criminal Procedure Code's rule that a statement is
-- admissible "to the extent that it is so admissible by this Code, the Evidence Act 1893, or any
-- other written law" (CPC s 268) points here.
--
-- The Act has no section on the voluntariness of a confession.  That is Criminal Procedure Code
-- section 258, which is not copied here; the answer it needs from this Act is the field
-- `the accused is entitled by section 122(4) ... to refuse to answer` and the others in
-- `Evidence Act Conclusions`, assembled in ea-conclusions.l4.

§ `17. Admission and confession defined`

-- 17(1): an admission is a statement, oral or documentary, which suggests an inference as to a
-- fact in issue or relevant fact, "and which is made by any of the persons and under the
-- circumstances hereinafter mentioned": that is, by a person in section 18, 19 or 20.  The three
-- sections are therefore the persons, and this rule is the shell they fill.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 17(1)"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement is an admission` IF
        a's `the statement is oral or documentary`
    AND a's `the statement suggests an inference as to a fact in issue or a relevant fact`
    AND (   `the maker is a person described in section 18(1)` a
         OR `the maker is a person described in section 18(3)` a
         OR `the maker is a person described in section 19` a
         OR `the maker is a person described in section 20` a)

-- 17(2): a confession is an admission made at any time by a person accused of an offence stating
-- or suggesting the inference that the person committed that offence.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 17(2)"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement is a confession` IF
        `the statement is an admission` a
    AND a's `the maker is a person accused of an offence`
    AND a's `the statement states or suggests the inference that the maker committed that offence`

§ `18 to 20. Whose statements are admissions`

-- 18(1) and 18(2) together: statements by a party, or by an authorised agent, are admissions; but
-- statements by a party suing or sued in a representative character are not admissions unless made
-- while the party held that character.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 18(1), (2)"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the maker is a person described in section 18(1)` IF
        (   a's `the maker is a party to the proceeding`
         OR a's `the maker is an agent of a party whom the court regards in the circumstances as expressly or impliedly authorised by the party to make the statement`)
    AND (   NOT a's `the maker is a party suing or sued in a representative character`
         OR a's `the maker held that representative character when making the statement`)

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 18(3)(a) and (b)"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the maker is a person described in section 18(3)` IF
        (   a's `the maker has a proprietary or pecuniary interest in the subject matter of the proceeding and made the statement in that character`
         OR a's `the maker is a person from whom a party to the suit derived the party's interest in the subject matter`)
    AND a's `the statement was made during the continuance of the maker's interest`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 19"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the maker is a person described in section 19` IF
        a's `the maker's position or liability must be proved as against a party to the suit`
    AND a's `the statement would be relevant as against the maker in relation to the position or liability in a suit brought by or against the maker`
    AND a's `the maker occupied the position or was subject to the liability when making the statement`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 20"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the maker is a person described in section 20` IF
    a's `the maker is a person to whom a party to the suit has expressly referred for information in reference to the matter in dispute`

§ `21 to 23 and 31. When an admission is relevant and may be proved`

-- 21: admissions are relevant and may be proved as against the maker or the maker's
-- representative in interest; they cannot be proved by or on behalf of the maker except in the
-- cases (a), (b) and (c).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 21(a), (b), (c)"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 21 permits the admission to be proved` IF
        a's `the admission is sought to be proved against the maker or the maker's representative in interest`
    OR  (   a's `the admission is sought to be proved by or on behalf of the maker or the maker's representative in interest`
        AND (   a's `the admission is of such a nature that, if the maker were dead, it would be relevant between third persons under section 32`
             OR a's `the admission is a statement of a state of mind or body, relevant or in issue, made at or about the time it existed and accompanied by conduct rendering its falsehood improbable`
             OR a's `the admission is relevant otherwise than as an admission`))

-- 22: an oral admission as to the contents of a document is not relevant unless the party proposing
-- to prove it shows entitlement to give secondary evidence of the contents, or the genuineness of a
-- document produced is in question.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 22"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 22 permits the admission to be proved` IF
        NOT a's `the admission is an oral admission as to the contents of a document`
    OR  a's `the party proposing to prove it shows that the party is entitled to give secondary evidence of the contents of the document`
    OR  a's `the genuineness of a document produced is in question`

-- 23(1): in civil cases no admission is relevant if made on an express condition that evidence of
-- it is not to be given, or in circumstances from which the court can infer an agreement that it
-- should not be.  23(2): the subsection does not exempt an advocate or solicitor, or an entity's
-- legal counsel, from giving evidence of a matter that may be compelled under section 128 or 128A.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 23(1) and (2)"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 23 excludes the admission` IF
        a's `the proceedings are civil proceedings`
    AND (   a's `the admission was made upon an express condition that evidence of it is not to be given`
         OR a's `the admission was made upon circumstances from which the court can infer that the parties agreed together that evidence of it should not be given`)
    AND NOT a's `the evidence is of a matter that an advocate or solicitor may be compelled to give evidence of under section 128`
    AND NOT a's `the evidence is of a matter that a legal counsel in an entity may be compelled to give evidence of under section 128A`

-- 31: admissions are not conclusive proof of the matters admitted, though they may operate as
-- estoppels.  That is a statement about weight and effect and decides nothing here.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 17 to 23"
GIVEN a IS AN `Admission Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the admission is relevant and may be proved` IF
        `the statement is an admission` a
    AND `section 21 permits the admission to be proved` a
    AND `section 22 permits the admission to be proved` a
    AND NOT `section 23 excludes the admission` a

§ `32. Statements of relevant facts by persons who are dead, cannot be found, etc.`

-- 32(1) chapeau: "statements of relevant facts made by a person (whether orally, in a document
-- or otherwise)".  Section 32A lets a protest, greeting or other verbal utterance be treated as
-- stating any fact it implies.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 32(1) and 32A"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement is a statement of a relevant fact by a person` IF
        h's `the statement was made by a person, whether orally, in a document or otherwise`
    AND (   h's `the statement is of a relevant fact`
         OR h's `the statement is a protest, greeting or other verbal utterance and the relevant fact is one the utterance implies`)

-- (a) cause of death.  The statement is relevant whether or not the maker was under expectation of
-- death, and whatever the nature of the proceeding in which the cause of death comes into question.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(a)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(a)` IF
        h's `the statement is made by a person as to the cause of that person's death, or as to a circumstance of the transaction that resulted in that person's death`
    AND h's `the cause of that person's death comes into question`

-- (b) ordinary course of trade, business, profession or other occupation.  The four things listed
-- after "in particular" (entries in books, receipts, market quotations, business records) are
-- illustrations of the general limb, not further conditions on it: see NOTES.md, fork F3.  The
-- closing words extend the limb to a statement in a record compiled in the ordinary course from
-- information supplied by others.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(b)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(b)` IF
        h's `the statement was made by a person in the ordinary course of a trade, business, profession or other occupation`
    OR  h's `the statement is made in a document that is, or forms part of, a record compiled by a person acting in the ordinary course of a trade, business, profession or other occupation on information supplied by other persons`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(c)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(c)` IF
        h's `the statement is against the pecuniary or proprietary interest of the person making it`
    OR  h's `the statement, if true, would expose or would have exposed the maker to a criminal prosecution or to a suit for damages`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(d)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(d)` IF
        h's `the statement gives the opinion of the maker as to the existence of a public right or custom or a matter of public or general interest`
    AND h's `the maker would have been likely to be aware of the existence of that right, custom or matter if it existed`
    AND h's `the statement was made before any controversy as to that right, custom or matter had arisen`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(e)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(e)` IF
        h's `the statement relates to the existence of a relationship by blood, marriage or adoption between persons as to whose relationship the maker had special means of knowledge`
    AND h's `the statement was made before the question in dispute was raised`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(f)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(f)` IF
        h's `the statement relates to the existence of a relationship by blood, marriage or adoption between deceased persons and is made in a will or deed relating to the affairs of their family, in a family pedigree, or upon a tombstone, family portrait or other thing on which such statements are usually made`
    AND h's `the statement was made before the question in dispute was raised`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(g)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(g)` IF
    h's `the statement is contained in a deed or other document relating to a transaction mentioned in section 13(a)`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(h)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(h)` IF
    h's `the statement was made by a number of persons and expressed feelings or impressions on their part relevant to the matter in question`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(i)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(i)` IF
        h's `the maker is compellable to give evidence on behalf of the party desiring to give the statement in evidence`
    AND h's `the maker attends or is brought before the court`
    AND (   h's `the maker refuses to be sworn or affirmed`
         OR h's `the maker is sworn or affirmed but refuses to give any evidence`)

-- (j): the four sub-paragraphs are alternatives joined by "or".
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(j)(i) to (iv)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(j)` IF
        h's `the maker is dead`
    OR  h's `the maker is unfit because of a bodily or mental condition to attend as a witness`
    OR  h's `despite reasonable efforts to locate the maker, the maker cannot be found, whether within or outside Singapore`
    OR  h's `the maker is outside Singapore and it is not practicable to secure the maker's attendance`
    OR  h's `the maker is competent but not compellable to give evidence on behalf of the party desiring to give the statement in evidence, and refuses to do so`

-- (k) agreement, with the two qualifications of subsections (6) and (7).
-- (6): despite (k), an agreement does not enable a statement to be given in evidence in criminal
-- proceedings on the prosecution's behalf unless, when the agreement is made, the accused or any
-- co-accused is represented by an advocate.
-- (7): despite (k), an agreement is of no effect for proceedings before the General Division of
-- the High Court, or arising out of them, if made during proceedings before an examining
-- Magistrate at a committal hearing under Division 2 of Part 10 of the Criminal Procedure Code
-- 2010 as in force immediately before 17 September 2018.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(k), (6) and (7)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within section 32(1)(k)` IF
        h's `the parties to the proceedings agree that for the purpose of those proceedings the statement may be given in evidence`
    AND NOT (   h's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
            AND h's `the statement is to be given in evidence on the prosecution's behalf`
            AND NOT h's `when the agreement was made the accused or a co-accused was represented by an advocate`)
    AND NOT (   h's `the agreement was made during proceedings before an examining Magistrate conducting a committal hearing under Division 2 of Part 10 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 as in force immediately before 17 September 2018`
            AND h's `the proceedings are before the General Division of the High Court or arise out of proceedings before it`)

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1)(a) to (j)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement falls within one of section 32(1)(a) to (j)` IF
        `the statement falls within section 32(1)(a)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(b)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(c)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(d)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(e)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(f)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(g)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(h)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(i)` h
    OR  `the statement falls within section 32(1)(j)` h

-- 32(4): except in the case of (k), evidence may not be given under subsection (1) on behalf of a
-- party unless that party complies, in criminal proceedings, with the notice requirements and other
-- conditions prescribed by the Minister under section 428 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 and, in
-- all other proceedings, with those prescribed in the Rules of Court or the Family Justice Rules.
-- The requirements themselves are in other instruments and are not encoded: whether they are
-- complied with is a fact, and for criminal proceedings it is the Code's conclusion.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(4)(a) and (b)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`, c IS A `Code Conclusions`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the notice requirements of section 32(4) are complied with` IF
    BRANCH IF h's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
           THEN c's `the notice requirements and other conditions prescribed under section 428 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 are complied with`
           OTHERWISE h's `the notice requirements and other conditions prescribed in the Rules of Court or the Family Justice Rules are complied with`

-- 32(3): a statement otherwise relevant under subsection (1) is not relevant if the court is of the
-- view that it would not be in the interests of justice to treat it as relevant.
-- 32(5): where a statement is admitted under subsection (1) the court assigns such weight as it
-- deems fit.  That is a matter of weight and is inert here.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(1), (3), (4), (6) and (7)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`, c IS A `Code Conclusions`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement is relevant under section 32(1)` IF
        `the statement is a statement of a relevant fact by a person` h
    AND (   (   `the statement falls within one of section 32(1)(a) to (j)` h
            AND `the notice requirements of section 32(4) are complied with` h c)
         OR `the statement falls within section 32(1)(k)` h)
    AND NOT h's `the court is of the view that it would not be in the interests of justice to treat the statement as relevant`

-- 32(2): where a person makes an oral statement to or in the hearing of another person who, at the
-- maker's request, puts it (or its substance) into writing at the time or reasonably soon afterwards,
-- the statement in the document is treated, for paragraphs (a), (c), (d), (e), (f), (g), (h), (i)
-- and (j), as the statement of the maker of the oral statement.  It is not so treated for (b) or (k).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32(2)"
GIVEN h IS A `Hearsay Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the written record is treated as the maker's statement for section 32(1)(a), (c) to (j)` IF
    h's `the oral statement was put by another person, at the maker's request, into writing at the time or reasonably soon afterwards`

§ `32B. Statements of opinion`

-- 32B(1): section 32 applies to statements of opinion as to statements of fact, subject to the
-- section.  32B(2): a statement of opinion is admissible under 32(1) only if it would be admissible
-- in those proceedings if made through direct oral evidence.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32B(1) and (2)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Statement Facts`, h IS A `Hearsay Facts`, c IS A `Code Conclusions`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement of opinion is relevant under section 32(1)` IF
        o's `the statement is a statement of opinion`
    AND o's `the statement would be admissible in those proceedings if made through direct oral evidence`
    AND `the statement is relevant under section 32(1)` h c

-- 32B(3): where a person is called as a witness, a statement of opinion by the person on a relevant
-- matter on which the person is not qualified to give expert evidence, if made as a way of conveying
-- relevant facts personally perceived, is admissible as evidence of what the person perceived.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32B(3)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the lay opinion is admissible as evidence of what the witness perceived` IF
        o's `the statement is a statement of opinion`
    AND o's `the maker is called as a witness in those proceedings`
    AND o's `the maker is not qualified to give expert evidence on the matter`
    AND o's `the opinion is made as a way of conveying relevant facts personally perceived by the maker`

§ `32C. Credibility of the maker of a statement admitted under section 32(1)`

-- 32C(1)(a): any evidence that would have been admissible to undermine or support the maker's
-- credibility had the maker been called is admissible for that purpose.  32C(1)(b): a matter that
-- could have been put in cross-examination for credibility, but of which evidence could not have been
-- adduced had it been denied, may be given in evidence with the court's permission.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32C(1)(a)"
GIVEN k IS A `Credibility of Absent Maker Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence going to the credibility of the absent maker is admissible under section 32C(1)(a)` IF
        k's `the statement is given in evidence by virtue of section 32(1)`
    AND k's `the maker of the statement is not called as a witness in the proceedings`
    AND k's `the evidence would have been admissible to undermine or support the maker's credibility had the maker been called`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32C(1)(b)"
GIVEN k IS A `Credibility of Absent Maker Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence going to the credibility of the absent maker is admissible under section 32C(1)(b)` IF
        k's `the statement is given in evidence by virtue of section 32(1)`
    AND k's `the maker of the statement is not called as a witness in the proceedings`
    AND k's `the matter could have been put to the maker in cross-examination for the purpose of undermining credibility, and evidence of it could not have been adduced by the cross-examining party if the maker had denied it`
    AND k's `the court gives permission for evidence of that matter`

-- 32C(2): evidence that the maker made another statement, before or after, inconsistent with the
-- statement admitted, is admissible to show that the maker contradicted himself or herself.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 32C(2)"
GIVEN k IS A `Credibility of Absent Maker Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence of an inconsistent statement by the absent maker is admissible under section 32C(2)` IF
        k's `the statement is given in evidence by virtue of section 32(1)`
    AND k's `the maker of the statement is not called as a witness in the proceedings`
    AND k's `the evidence tends to prove that the maker made, before or after the statement, another statement inconsistent with it`

-- 32C(3) extends (1) and (2), for section 32(1)(b), to the person who originally supplied the
-- information from which the statement was made as well as the maker; 32C(4) applies section 32(2) for
-- the purposes of section 32C as it applies for section 32(1).  Both are extensions of the persons
-- and the form of statement to which the rules above apply, not further conditions, and are stated
-- here rather than as rules.

§ `33. Evidence given in an earlier proceeding`

-- 33: evidence given by a witness in a judicial proceeding, or before a person authorised by law to
-- take it, is relevant to prove, in a later proceeding or a later stage of the same one, the truth of
-- the facts it states, when the witness is dead, cannot be found, is incapable of giving evidence, is
-- kept out of the way by the adverse party, or the witness's presence cannot be obtained without
-- unreasonable delay or expense, subject to provisos (a) to (c).  The Explanation deems a criminal
-- trial or inquiry a proceeding between the prosecutor and the accused: see NOTES.md, fork F4.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 33(a), (b), (c) and Explanation"
GIVEN f IS A `Former Testimony Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the earlier evidence is relevant under section 33` IF
        f's `the evidence was given by a witness in a judicial proceeding or before a person authorised by law to take it`
    AND (   f's `the witness is dead`
         OR f's `the witness cannot be found`
         OR f's `the witness is incapable of giving evidence`
         OR f's `the witness is kept out of the way by the adverse party`
         OR f's `the court considers that the witness's presence cannot be obtained without an amount of delay or expense that is unreasonable in the circumstances`)
    AND (   f's `the proceeding was between the same parties or their representatives in interest`
         OR f's `the earlier proceeding was a criminal trial or inquiry and the later proceeding is between the same prosecutor and accused`)
    AND f's `the adverse party in the first proceeding had the right and opportunity to cross-examine`
    AND f's `the questions in issue were substantially the same in the first as in the second proceeding`
